  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 109 PM CST Mon Nov 16 2026\n\nThe early afternoon water vapor and RAP objective analysis fields \nrevealed a ridge of high pressure in the middle/upper troposphere \ncentered across the High Plains with dominant polar jet stream \nextending from California northeastward across Northern Rockies of \nwestern Montana. Through much of this forecast period (at least \nthrough Friday), the prevailing storm track will be from the \nsouthwest to the northeast from California across the Northern \nRockies and adjacent northern Great Plains. As such, sensible \nweather will not change much from initial conditions other than a \nfurther increase in wind speed Tuesday and especially Wednesday \nalong with a gradual warm-up in temperature through mid-week. As a \nstrong MSLP gradient will be a feature across western/central Kansas \nthrough the week, wind speeds will be fairly strong.\n\nExpect Tuesday and Wednesday to be particularly windy in advance of \nthe first shortwave trough with both days showing probs of 45+ mph \nwind gusts in the 60-90% range for the northwestern one-half to two-\nthirds of the NWS DDC forecast area. As the first shortwave trough \nlifts northeast late Wednesday/Wednesday Night, the leeside surface \ntrough axis will push eastward enough such that south/southwest \nwinds on Thursday will not be quite as strong owing to a more \nrelaxed MSLP gradient. This initial system will be followed quickly \nby a second larger wave. This will follow in the footsteps of the \nfirst storm, lifting northeast quickly across the Northern/Central \nHigh Plains Friday. The shortwave trough will lift out as an open \nwave with greatest deep tropospheric lift displaced well to the \nnorth of western Kansas. Surface low development initially just east \nof the Front Range of Colorado will shift northeast along the front \nacross northern Nebraska. This setup does not favor organized \nshower/thunderstorm activity across western Kansas in mid-November. \nThis drying trend for western Kansas continues in the models and \nPOPs for Friday Night continue to drop as a result. Latest forecast \nwill now call for only 10 to 20 POPs with the highest of these \nlowered POPs focused across the Hays area (albeit still less than \n25%). This is quite a shift in the forecast from just 24 hours ago \nwhen NBM (and official forecast) had 40-50% POPs for much of western \nand central Kansas east of US-83.\n\nThis pattern will also favor continued above normal temperatures \nwith daily highs Wednesday through Friday in the 60s and lows same \nperiod from the mid 40s to even some lower 50s early Friday morning. \nThe next reasonable chance for widespread temperatures 30 degrees or \nless will be next Sunday morning 22nd (current NBM probs of 30 \ndegrees or less at 50-80%). It should be noted that the 30-Year \nclimatological low for November 22nd at Dodge City is 28 degrees. A \npattern change this upcoming weekend should put southwest Kansas \ncloser to consistent climatological lows in the upper 20s to lower \n30s.",
